  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nAnother warm early September morning with temperatures in the 60s\nto lower 70s. Patchy fog developed in some spots and will monitor\nthe lakeshore where some patchy dense fog may be possible on Lake\nMichigan where recent upwelling has cooled off water \ntemperatures. \n\nComplex forecast this morning dealing with potential for strong\nor severe thunderstorms at times through late tonight. First\nround of thunderstorms expected to move across the area this\nmorning into early this afternoon. Looking at two signals for\nthis to happen. Latest models indicated scattered thunderstorms \nwill develop between 08z and 11z across central into east-central \nWI in response to a 40 knot/850 warm advection on a low level jet.\nAlso watching convection developing across southern Minnesota and\nnorthern Iowa that will make its way eastward into our area later\nthis morning. Bufkit soundings at Green Bay indicated 40-50 knots\nof 0-6 km shear, surface base CAPE of 1,000-1,500 J/Kg with most \nunstable CAPE values around 2,000 J/KG. Some of the storms could \nbecome strong or severe with damaging winds, large hail and \ntorrential rainfall this morning. \n\nThere will likely be a lull or break in the thunderstorm activity\nthis afternoon. With the best instability to our south, scattered\nthunderstorms may redevelop during the peak heating of the day\nacross central into east-central WI closer to the best instability.\nSome of these storms could become severe as well. The plot \nthickens tonight with a complex of thunderstorms developing \nacross the Upper Midwest. The latest Canadian/ECMWF/NAM/GFS model\nwould suggest a complex of thunderstorms would work into the area\nduring the late evening and overnight hours. However, there are a\nfew meso models would suggest development would occur during the \nevening closer to the better instability and then drop south into \nsouthern WI. \n\nThere are periodic chances of thunderstorms while above normal \ntemperatures will continue Friday into early next week as the \nsummer time pattern continues.",
